- **`adapters/agents/src/mcp/adapter.ts` +
  `MCP-ENTRYPOINT.md` (generated)** — novel to AIECP. The MCP
  (Model Context Protocol) agent adapter declares capabilities
  mediated by MCP servers (filesystem server, shell server,
  test-runner server): `filesystem_read: true`, `filesystem_write:
  true`, `shell_exec: true`, `test_runner: true`, `native_skills:
  true` (MCP agents read SKILL.md via the filesystem server), `mcp:
  true` (the defining capability), `browser: false` (MCP does not
  standardize browser access as of 2026-08-15 per web research),
  `sandboxed_code_execution: false` (MCP actions are real host
  mutations, not ephemeral sandbox writes — unlike the
  `chat-sandbox` adapter). Per web research 2026-08-15: MCP is
  now a Linux Foundation standard under the Agentic AI Foundation,
  with 10,000+ active public servers and 41% of surveyed
  organizations in production use. The protocol itself is
  vendor-neutral (multiple independent client implementations:
  Claude Code, Cursor, Continue, Cline, Zed, the Eclipse IDE
  family, Codex, OpenCode) — no per-vendor attribution is needed
  for the protocol's existence or shape. The adapter-specific
  `translateObservation` mapping (server-name extraction from
  `obs.raw.server` / `obs.raw.server_name` / `obs.raw.tool` and
  the `source: "mcp:<server_name>"` prefix convention) and the
  `MCP-ENTRYPOINT.md` template (30-second orientation, server
  discovery section, evidence protocol section, tool integration
  table mapping each capability to a typical MCP server) are
  AIECP-original, derived from the same `AgentAdapter` interface
  and `AgentCapabilities` type that the `claude-code` / `codex` /
  `chat` / `chat-sandbox` adapters already implement. No upstream
  code or prose was adapted or copied; the safety-gate
  (`aiecp:confirm`) requirement for the MCP adapter is
  emphasized because MCP-mediated destructive operations persist
  on the real host filesystem (unlike chat-sandbox's ephemeral
  writes).

