  the procedure wrapping it is AIECP-original), defines MVP scope
  (in / out / Phase 2+), identifies 1-3 user personas, and
  suggests monetization angles. The five-dimension elicitation
  framework (target platform, user type, scale, monetization
  model, timeline) and the verbatim-capture-first rule (step 1,
  mirroring `systematic-debugging`'s "locate evidence before
  forming a theory") are AIECP-original, derived from the
  Evidence Model's `Decision`/`Trace`/`Event` entities and
  `constitution/constitution.md` §4 (question economy). No
  upstream code or prose was adapted or copied. The skill fills
  the "requirements" gap in AIECP's SDLC coverage (see
  `skills/README.md`'s "Planning skills (SDLC gap-fill)"
  section); prior to this skill, the catalog went directly from
  `project-onboarding` (technical discovery of an existing repo)
  to `specification` (authoring behavioral contracts) with no
  step for discovering what the human actually wants.

- **`skills/project-planning/SKILL.md`** — novel to AIECP. The
  decomposition layer: converts `specs/requirements.md` (from
  `requirements-gathering`) into a phased development plan with
  modular task breakdown, dependency graph (ASCII-rendered,
  surfacing the critical path), risk assessment (3-7 risks with
  concrete mitigations, not "hope it doesn't happen"), and the
  independent-testability rule (each module's tests run in
  isolation, not only as part of the full suite — making the
  dependency graph honest about cross-task test-time
  dependencies). The LIVING PLAN rule — re-invoked on the
  orchestrator's `plan_revision_needed` transition, bounded by
  the `plan_revision_limit_reached` three-failure cap mirroring
  `systematic-debugging` Phase 4.5 — is AIECP-original, derived
  from the orchestrator's back-edge pattern
  (`evaluate-result → route`) applied to the planning/architecture
  boundary rather than the workflow-execution boundary. The phase
  decomposition convention (Phase 0 Foundation → Phase 1 MVP →
  Phase 2 v1.0 → ...) is widely-used in software engineering; the
  specific procedure wrapping it (the file-level contract with
  `specs/plan.md` + `specs/tasks.md`, the append-not-overwrite
  rule per ADR-0002 "plan accrues", the `Decision` with `what:
  "plan_created"` / `what: "plan_revised:<reason>"`) is
  AIECP-original. No upstream code or prose was adapted or copied.

- **`skills/architecture-design/SKILL.md`** — novel to AIECP.
  The technical-decision layer: selects technology stack,
  designs system architecture (monolith/microservice/serverless
  with the modular-monolith default unless a genuine forcing
  function is present), database schema, API contracts, and
  deployment topology. The conflict-detection step (step 7) —
  emitting a `Decision` with `what:
  "architecture_constraint_conflict"` when architectural
  constraints conflict with requirements, triggering the
  orchestrator's `plan_revision_needed` feedback loop — is
  AIECP-original and the structural feature that makes planning
  + architecture a co-evolution rather than a waterfall. The
  encoding of the logical `conflicts_with_requirements: true`
  signal in the `what` field (rather than as a separate boolean
  field) is a deliberate schema-stability choice:
  `evidence/schema/decision.schema.json`'s
  `additionalProperties: false` forbids adding new fields, so
  the conflict flag is encoded in the `what` string for the
  orchestrator to match on. The recency-verification discipline
  for stack-version claims (per `constitution/constitution.md`
  §8 and `skills/recency-verification/SKILL.md`) is cited, not
  re-implemented. No upstream code or prose was adapted or
  copied. The skill writes `specs/architecture.md` (new file,
  not in the original ADR-0002 spec-kit family — added by this
  skill as the architectural analogue of `specs/plan.md`), plus
  fills `specs/contracts.md` and `specs/invariants.md` from
  their existing ADR-0002 templates (the templates are spec-kit's
  verbatim upstream artifacts per ADR-0018 and are NOT modified
  by this skill — only filled with project-specific content).

- **`skills/ux-design/SKILL.md`** — novel to AIECP. The
  design-decision layer: designs user flows (entry → key actions
  → exit per persona, per user story), wireframes (text-based
  ASCII art — deliberately NOT image wireframes, because text
  wireframes are diff-able in git, accessible to screen-reader-
  using designers, and schema-checkable via `Expected` with
  `source_ref` pointing at `specs/ux/wireframes.md#Wireframe-N`),
  journey maps (the emotional/functional journey table surfacing
  pain points and opportunities per-screen wireframes miss), and
  design system basics (color palette with WCAG contrast ratios
  verified at design time per `skills/frontend/SKILL.md` step 8,
  typography with modular type scale, spacing with base unit +
  scale, component library basics). The text-wireframe choice
  (over Figma/Sketch images) is AIECP-original and motivated by
  the Evidence Model's `source_ref` requirement: a wireframe that
  cannot be cited by an `Expected` is a design no `verify` state
  can validate against. The accessibility-at-design-time rule
  (each interactive element in each wireframe has an accessible
  name, role, and keyboard-reachability note — not deferred to
  `frontend` implementation) is AIECP-original, derived from
  `skills/frontend/SKILL.md` step 3's accessibility-invariant
  `Expected` discipline pulled upstream to design time. No
  upstream code or prose was adapted or copied.

- **`workflows/orchestrator.sm.yaml` (plan_revision_needed +
  plan_revision_limit_reached transitions) and
  `skills/orchestrator/SKILL.md` (project_scale detection +
  LIVING PLAN feedback loop + three-failure cap)** — novel to
  AIECP, extending the existing orchestrator (already attributed
  above). Two new transitions: `evaluate-result → route on:
  plan_revision_needed` (the LIVING PLAN back-edge, structurally
  parallel to the existing `goal_not_yet_met` back-edge but
  triggered by `architecture-design`'s `architecture_constraint_
  conflict` Decision rather than a remaining sub-goal) and
  `evaluate-result → blocked on: plan_revision_limit_reached`
  (the three-failure cap, mirroring `systematic-debugging`'s
  Phase 4.5 three-failure rule — three rejected architectural
  candidates is the empirical signal that the requirements/
  architecture conflict is structural, not local). The
  `classify-goal` state's new `project_scale: small|medium|large`
  Decision (with inspected signals: word count, `--yarat` flag,
  multi-platform mention — per `tool-use-discipline` and
  constitution §8, derived from inspection not recall) drives
  routing: `small` skips all four planning skills; `medium`
  invokes only `requirements-gathering`; `large` invokes the
  full planning chain. No upstream code or prose was adapted or
  copied; the three-failure rule is cited by reference to
  `skills/systematic-debugging/SKILL.md` (which itself adapts the
  concept from obra/superpowers, already attributed above).
